  "post": {
    "caption": "Venice's doge married the sea. Each Ascension Day, the doge threw a gold ring into the sea. He rode out on a gilded state barge called the Bucintoro. A bishop waited in a gilded boat to bless him. The ring was blessed before it went into the water. He dropped it where the lagoon meets the open sea. In Latin, he said, we wed thee, sea. The ring was a sign of lasting dominion over the sea. After Napoleon ended the Republic, the barge was burned for its gold. Since 1965, the mayor of Venice repeats it each Ascension Day.",
